s  genomes we've known that since like  1960s but it's been debated whether or  not natural selection ever exploits that  fact and right here in 2004 this paper  says that yes natural selection does  exploit this and they gave evidence  pretty good evidence suggesting that in  humans our most important genes happen  to be located in the safest spots of our  genomes and last week i talked about a  mechanism that could cause that so if we  already knew this then what does this  new paper really even tell us well first  off they did huge real-time experiments  on mutation rates that paper from 2004  that convinced me that i needed to edit  my video my biology professor was  convinced of that as well but not  everyone in the scientific community was  this new paper what they did is they  performed a huge real-time experiment on  mutation rates they looked at how  mutations happen in real time to make  sure that important genes really were  mutating less than the rest of the  genome second they proposed a mechanism  they gave pretty good evidence showing  how it is that they think cells are  actually doing this physically and then  finally they demonstrated mathematically  that normal natural selection can easily  cause what they found so what was their  proposed mechanism last week i talked  about translocation mutations and  transposition mutations we've long  suspected that these types of mutations  these are types of mutations that take  chunks of dna and move them to new spots  in the genome if these are happening  just by accident all over the place if  one of these translocation mutations  happens to move a really important gene  into a safer spot of the genome  the mother who has that mutation when  she has offspring she's less likely to  have miscarriages and therefore natural  selection can promote that mutation that  translocation mutation that's very  likely one of the important mechanisms  that allows genomes to evolve in this  way but the new mechanism that was  proposed it's not totally new it's  actually 10 years old but they  they found a lot of supporting evidence  for this dna is not just loosely hanging  out inside of a cell it's wrapped around  protein complexes called histones and  when a gene gets used by the cell a  bunch of different proteins clamp onto  it and they make a copy of it into rna  and then that rna is used for something  maybe it becomes a functional part of a  ribosome or maybe it's used to code for  protein  but when a gene is being used  histones are chemically modified and  they're chemically modified in a  reversible way little molecules get  stuck to them and we call these  epigenetic markers and because these  epigenetic markers get stuck to genes  that are being used a lot researchers  who worked on this new paper 10 years  ago they suggested that maybe some of  our dna repair proteins the proteins  that scan dna for  little nicks and breaks and fix them  maybe some of those have evolved the  ability to recognize these little  markers maybe they stick to them and if  so  that would mean  that genes that are used more often get  more protection from the cell they have  more access to these repair mechanisms  they suggested that 10 years ago and  then they found evidence for it here in  this paper pretty strong evidence that's  really really cool  and then finally they demonstrated  mathematically that normal natural  selection can easily cause what they  found and that demonstration was  actually 10 years ago in the 2012 paper  this is the paper non-random mutation  the evolution of targeted hypermutation  and hypomutation and that was a really  important paper because there were  debates from people who work strictly in  theory in these mathematical models of  evolution suggesting that even though  there are variable mutation rates  in the genomes of organisms there  probably isn't enough selection pressure  to allow natural selection to take  advantage of this fact this paper 10  years ago was a rebuttal to that they  said actually this could happen  especially if you had a mechanism that  allowed genes that are used a lot to  have more access the cell's repair  systems you could easily get the  evolution of hypomutation so slowed down  mutation in  essential genes so that is what this  paper has found it's a really cool paper  most of what's here is is not new  especially it's not completely new but  they really did demonstrate once and for  all i mean it's very very hard to argue  with the data that they've presented yes  not only does natural selection weed out  mutations when they do happen in  essential genes but mutations actually  happen less often and this protection  evolved it evolved through the normal  process of descent with modification  acted upon by natural selection they  have this gem of a line here right  before the conclusion they say while  perhaps initially surprising our  synthesis between epigenomics so those  little tags that i was telling you about  and population genetic theory predicts  that the observed bias could readily  arise  via natural selection  before closing  i want to  point out something that  they talked about really briefly they  barely touched on it in this paper i  think they're probably going to publish  a paper that goes way more in depth  later but i want to point something out  about this system that they found this  mechanism that they found these  epigenetic markers  these changes in the histones that  happen when a gene is used a lot those  are  temporary in genes that are constantly  being used those are constantly being  put back on them those tags are  but in genes that are not used very  often those will eventually dissipate  what this means  is that if for multiple generations a  specific gene is not being used the cell  will stop protecting that gene from  mutation  basically what these people have  discovered is a real-time sensor which  tattletails on jeans for slacking off  freeloaders get mutated  that is really cool that is really cool  i hope that they continue investigating  down this line  i'm curious to see how the dynamics of  this system play out in real life  with what i just told you there i don't  know how  that would translate back into the  germline very well in fact i don't know  that it would at all maybe it wouldn't  at all maybe this would only be  beneficial in single-celled organisms  but  super interesting to think about  so  there you have it folks our mutations  random  yes they are random in the sense that  with very very few exceptions  mutations do not happen quote on purpose  as gigi simpson said heat induced  mutations do not produce phenotypic  change related to heat tolerance  they just produce change as dobzhansky  said an ideal situation would be if the  organism were to respond to the  challenge of the changing environment by  producing only beneficial mutations  where and when needed but nature has not  been kind enough to endow creations with  such a providential ability  so far as we know those claims are still  true  there are a few exceptions like crispr  that violate those claims but they are  very very few mutations are random and  that they do not happen quote on purpose  however they are not completely random  and that they don't happen  perfectly randomly in any location in  the genome and natural selection has  taken advantage of this there are kings  and there are pawns  in the genome  aki  i hope that answers your question  so long for now
 

